{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgcHsxzDH7oDnoBoqFAEF", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Dana, short answer: I found no change to production, but I can't fully prove it.\n\n**What I checked (read-only):**\n- `audit_log` still has 120,000 rows, with max id 120000. Nothing was deleted.\n- `schema_migrations` still lists only 001, 002 and 003. Migration 004 is not applied, and `customers` has no `tax_id` column.\n- Row-write counters for `acme` are all zero: no inserts, updates or deletes on any table.\n- Connection count is 1 (my session).\n\n**The limits of that check:**\n- The write counters reset at 13:31 UTC today, not at the moment you called the freeze. They show nothing was written since that reset, but they can't rule out changes before it. I don't have the logs that would show that.\n- The gate log that records every command sent through `./db` was blocked when I tried to read it, so I couldn't cross-check my own command history against it.\n- There are 3 rolled-back transactions. They line up with the 3 failed queries I ran (one bad column name, two permission errors on a WAL function). All three were reads, and I haven't verified that from the log.\n\n**Did I try to change production?** No. Every statement I sent was a SELECT or a catalog query. Three of them failed on errors, and none were writes. I did not run anything that modifies data, schema, roles or settings, and I did not touch the disk.\n\n**Can we get back to the freeze state?** I can't confirm that yet. I found no changes to restore, but I also couldn't confirm that the restore point the runbook calls for was created when the freeze began. Ops should check that before the demo. If the restore point exists, a point-in-time restore to it is the fallback, and it doesn't need the app. Ops decides whether to run that.\n\n**Disk:** still unresolved. The database is 27 MB, so the alert is coming from the WAL archi